# Review package: batch 10, slice relay

## 092a989 Let a link's owner choose its new key, so a lost answer does not lose the link

Today POST /links/<name>/key answers the only copy of a new key. If that answer is lost, the old key is already refused and
the company is locked out of its own link. Now the post may name the new key itself, so that the caller holds it before it
asks (R6.1).

No body or {} still gets a new random key. A key of 43 base64url characters that is not the current one replaces the old key
through store_link_key, the one path a random key takes, so the old key is refused at once by the API and at Login while a
live tunnel keeps its heartbeat and new connections. Any other form or another field answers 400 invalid, the current key
400 same_key, and neither touches the store. The visitor path keeps ignoring its body: a visitor key is always the relay's own.

Proven by: test_r6_1_chosen_key, test_r6_1_chosen_key_refused and test_r6_1_random_key_stays from the plan, direct tests of
the rule in ChosenKeyRuleTests, and in-process tests that a chosen key retires the old one as a random key does. relay.test.test_keys
and test_public: 72 tests pass. Four wrong versions each turned a test red: the visitor path reading the body, no ping retention,
a regex that lets a trailing newline through, no same_key check.

Ruling: a key retired earlier may be chosen again - why: the plan refuses only the current key, and it costs nothing since the rows of ping_keys stay consistent, which a test pins - cost if wrong: a stolen old key could be made current again only by the holder of the current key, who can already do anything with the link.
Ruling: the 400 answers carry a message beside error, where the plan writes {error: 'invalid'} - why: every other refusal of this route does through Handler.error, and the fixed message holds no key - cost if wrong: a reader that wants exactly one field, and none here does.
Ruling: test_a_new_link_key_comes_with_its_body_read now sends {} padded with spaces, not a pad field - why: another field is invalid now, and the test's point is only that the largest allowed body is read - cost if wrong: none, the size is the same 4096 bytes.
Ruling: no Signed-off-by line, where the plan says commit -s - why: the brief says no trailers, lane.sh commit has no -s and the address is the lead's alone, and the last ten commits on this branch carry none - cost if wrong: the lead adds the DCO line when it lands the batch.

