## baf4576 Make an operator's add wait for a freed name's old tunnel, as the public API does

admin_add asked only check_name, and hornhoof-relay did not load /etc/hornhoof-relay.env, so relay.py had no password for frps's
dashboard. An operator who freed a name and ran `hornhoof-relay add <name>` while the old owner's http-mode tunnel was connected
sent that name's visitors to the old owner: an http route gets no NewUserConn, and frps refuses the new owner's frpc while the old
one holds the route. Batch 7's delta review, Important 1.

admin_add now asks requested_name_reason, as POST /bindings does (R1.5): a route frps shows or has yet to show reads as taken.
While frps cannot tell, it says so on stderr and exits 1, which stops hornhoof-relay under set -e. hornhoof-relay sources the env
file when it can read it.

test_r7_4_a_new_owner_never_opens_the_old_owners_tunnel now puts its second binding straight into SQLite, since an add no longer
can, and test_admin_unban_placeholder_removes_credential_and_old_visitor_access binds through the store, since it has no frps to ask.

Proven by: a real frps with an http-mode tunnel in test_r1_5_an_operators_add_waits_for_the_tunnel_too, in-process cases for each
answer, and hornhoof-relay's own text against a stand-in dashboard that wants the env file's password. Each failed first, on the
add handing out the name; with the source line disabled the last one fails with `cannot tell`.

Ruling: an add of any name refuses while frps cannot tell, also one never bound - why: the brief's exit 1 on FrpsUnknown, and the relay cannot know that a name has no route when frps does not answer, as POST /bindings answers 503 for a named binding - cost if wrong: an operator waits for frps to answer before adding a name.

