## 1ab6550 Count the bytes the relay sends each month

Every minute the relay reads the tx_bytes counter of the default route's interface and adds what was sent since the
last read to the UTC month's total in SQLite. A reboot, another interface or a counter that went down counts the whole
reading from zero; the first reading adds nothing. The two writes are one transaction, so a crash cannot count bytes
twice. Past 300 GB in a month one line a day goes to the journal, from a round function that tests drive with fake
proc and sys folders. `hornhoof-relay traffic` prints the last 12 months, oldest first.

Ruling: the README sentence goes right after the "For each address" paragraph of "What it stores" - why: two other
paragraphs there also mention addresses, and this one is the one about addresses kept - cost if wrong: one sentence moved.
Ruling: a first read of a month that adds nothing writes no row - why: the table then lists only months with traffic -
cost if wrong: a month shows no 0.0 GB line.
Lock order: these writes take neither create_lock nor route_lock, only the write queue through transaction().

